as we enter the final weeks of summer  inflation lingers supply chain delays  continue and interest rates are up while  an official recession has not been  declared these factors can make one feel  imminent there are a number of other  signals that while unusual are also  warnings a recession could be on the way  google search trends  between july 24th and 30th the same week  it was reported that u.s gdp shrank for  a second consecutive quarter online  searches for recession exploded  according to google trends the term was  searched more in that week of july than  any other week since 2004. lemon.io a  marketplace of vetted software  developers analyzed google search trends  and also found searches for will i lose  my job in a recession are up nine  thousand nine hundred percent searches  for what to do when you get laid off are  up three hundred and thirty six percent  and searches for lost my job can't pay  rent have risen 467  mlb attendance  attendance at games through the first  half of the 2022 season was down at 23  of 30 ballparks compared to 2019 game  attendance overall has been on a  downward trend even before the pandemic  but luxury and recreational purchases  like game tickets are typically some of  the first items consumers cut down on  when the economy cools down according to  simasha  chief strategist at principal global  investors but shaw also warns a problem  with this data at the moment is it's  hard to separate what is a post-pandemic  fear of large crowds from economic  trends  private label sales consumers of all  income levels are increasingly swapping  out name brand products for less  expensive generic substitutes analytics  firm in market reported 46 of  respondents to a survey in june said  they're turning more to store brand  products than before this also includes  gen z buyers whose spending on private  labels grew nearly 36 between february  and march this year and finally believe  it or not  men's underwear can also be an indicator  back in 2008 former federal reserve  chairman alan greenspan told npr that  men's underwear sales is one way to tell  how an economy is doing as it is  typically one of the last items of  clothing people look to buy if there's a  dip in sales this could mean wallets are  stretched thin as household budgets are  more focused on food and gas  [Music]  you
 

